<?xml version="1.0"?>
<model>
  <name>continuum_arm_cosserat</name>
  <version>1.0</version>
  <sdf version='1.9'>model.sdf</sdf>

  <author>
   <name>Aabhash Bhandari</name>
  </author>

  <description>
    3-tendon soft continuum robotic arm: Ø40mm OD / Ø30mm ID lattice-TPU
    tube (behaves like a traditional silicone continuum arm), mounted on a
    cylindrical adapter base (radius 0.04m, height 0.02m). The SAME physical
    arm as continuum_arm_pcc (identical geometry, mass, and actuation
    limits), the 0.40m tube is discretized into 15 rigid segments for
    visualization/actuation (solid-cylinder visual/collision at the 20mm
    outer radius, but mass/inertia computed from the true hollow-tube
    cross-section), driven by a dynamic Cosserat rod mechanics solver
    (CosseratRodSolver, in plugins/cosserat_arm_controller) instead of
    continuum_arm_pcc's piecewise-constant-curvature (uniform angle split)
    model.

    The solver integrates the classic Cosserat statics ODEs (linear
    constitutive law, Kse=diag(GA,GA,EA), Kbt=diag(EIxx,EIyy,GJ), with A,
    Ixx, Iyy, J computed from the TRUE annulus (hollow tube) cross-section -
    not a solid rod - and E=5e5 Pa, G=1.6e5 Pa) via a shooting method: RK4
    integration from base to tip plus Newton-Raphson correction of the base
    wrench (n0, m0) against the tip boundary condition, converting a
    commanded 3-tendon length vector into pulling tensions and, from those,
    the equilibrium backbone shape - including a physically correct tapered
    curvature (more bend near the base than the tip) rather than PCC's
    uniform split.

    Material: E=5e5 Pa, G=1.6e5 Pa, effective density ~780 kg/m^3 (~65% of
    solid TPU's ~1200 kg/m^3, reflecting lattice infill). An Ashby
    bending-dominated-lattice estimate (E_eff ~= E_solid*rel_density^2) with
    a soft printable TPU (E_solid~1.2MPa) at this relative density lands
    almost exactly on 5e5 Pa, consistent with "behaves like a silicone soft
    arm" (typical silicone Young's modulus is 0.05-0.5 MPa).

    Mass budget: total assembly (base + 15 segments) is ~250g (m_base~78.4g
    + 15*m_seg~11.4g), using a uniform effective density of ~780 kg/m^3
    across base and tube alike - see gen_cosserat_arm.py.

    Actuation: 3 tendons routed along the inner wall's 30mm-diameter circle
    (pitch radius d=0.015m = ID/2), 120deg apart, pulled by a 35kgcm servo
    (3.43 N*m stall torque - through any realistic winch-drum radius this
    delivers well over 100N of tendon tension, so the servo is never the
    binding actuation constraint here). The plugin's max_tension clamp
    (16N) is instead set by this rod's own mechanics: at E=5e5 Pa and this
    cross-section, its Euler buckling load is under 1N, so tendon
    tension-vs-bend is a genuine (non-buggy) large-deflection elastica
    response - non-monotonic once several N past that threshold, not a
    simple "more tension = more bend" curve. Every tendon carries a
    tendon_baseline pretension (5.5N) even when un-shortened, both
    matching how a real cable-driven arm is actually operated (tendons
    kept taut, not slack) and moving the operating point onto a much
    larger clean, reversal-free branch of that curve (~10.5N span above
    baseline, ~0.19rad) than pulling from a fully-slack rest state would
    allow (~0.1rad). Tendon force/moment is applied as a concentrated
    boundary condition at the tip (where the tendons actually terminate),
    not smeared as a distributed load along the whole rod. See
    CosseratRodSolver.hpp/CosseratArmController.hh for the full
    derivation and CosseratArmController's effort_limit (0.24 N*m =
    max_tension * pitch_radius) for the matching PD-force actuator cap,
    shared with continuum_arm_pcc's own effort_limit.

    Because PCC's kinematic model has no notion of axial tendon load (and
    so no buckling behavior at all), its own reachable single-tendon bend
    range is larger than Cosserat's - a genuine difference between the
    two simulation approaches at this material's stiffness, not a tuning
    gap between them.

    Actuated by the CosseratArmController model plugin, which subscribes to
    /continuum_arm_cosserat/tendon_cmd (gz.msgs.Vector3d, x=l1, y=l2, z=l3),
    runs the solve, and drives each of the 15 universal joints toward the
    resulting per-segment relative-rotation target with a PD force loop
    (joints here carry no SDF spring dynamics - elasticity lives entirely
    in the solver's own constitutive law). Segments are shaded amber-to-
    orange (base to tip) to stay visually distinct from continuum_arm_pcc's
    blue.

    Publishes actual tip pose on /continuum_arm_cosserat/tip_pose and joint
    states (stock JointStatePublisher) on /continuum_arm_cosserat/joint_states.

    Mount contract: continuum_arm_cosserat/base_cyl's top face (z=0 in this
    module's own frame) is the attachment interface - same contract as
    continuum_arm_pcc, meant for a bottom-mounted glue joint from a
    composing vehicle model.
  </description>
</model>
